# ArtLight Server - the keyboards that arrive over USB/IP, and nothing else.
#
# Why this exists
# ---------------
# When a keyboard is shared into this machine over USB/IP it arrives on the vhci_hcd bus,
# which means its sysfs path contains "vhci_hcd" and every keyboard built into the machine
# does not. That is the whole test.
#
# It is used to read the shared keyboard and watch for the combination that ends a stream.
# The alternative was to put the server in the "input" group, which would let it read every
# keyboard on the machine - far more access than this needs. This rule grants access to the
# one device that matters, at the moment it appears, and to nothing else.
#
# Measured, not assumed
# ---------------------
# On the machine this was written against:
#   /sys/bus/platform/devices/vhci_hcd.0   ->  DEVPATH=/devices/platform/vhci_hcd.0
#   /sys/class/input/event0                ->  /devices/pci0000:00/0000:00:1f.0/.../input0
#   /sys/class/input/event1                ->  /devices/platform/PNP0C0E:00/input/input1
#   /sys/class/input/event2                ->  /devices/platform/PNP0C0C:00/input/input2
# A device attached over USB/IP is enumerated below vhci_hcd.0, so its path reads
# /devices/platform/vhci_hcd.0/usbN/.../input/inputN/eventN - it matches, and none of the
# built-in keyboards do.
#
# The match itself was proved with udevadm test before this rule was written: the pattern
# below fires on the vhci_hcd device and on no built-in device or input node.
#
# 70- on purpose: 50-udev-default.rules sets GROUP="input" on input nodes, and a later file
# wins, so this rule takes effect over it.
#
# It applies to the group the ArtLight package already creates; no new group is added here.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", DEVPATH=="*/vhci_hcd.[0-9]*", GROUP="artlight-uinput", MODE="0660"
